<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?>
<!--
  Why this file exists: without a Common-Controls v6 dependency every standard
  control in this process is drawn by comctl32 v5, the Windows 95 look. The
  font was right, the sizes were right, and the buttons still looked like
  1995, which is what a person reported as "the buttons are ugly".

  **DPI awareness is deliberately NOT declared here.** main.rs calls
  SetProcessDpiAwarenessContext(PER_MONITOR_AWARE_V2) at startup, and when
  both a manifest and a call say something about it **the manifest wins and
  the call fails, returning false, saying nothing**. One place says it, and
  it is that one. If you are here to declare it in this file, delete that call
  in the same edit; leaving both is how the code stops meaning anything while
  still being read.

  This file contains no DPI element of any kind, and the element's name is
  deliberately not spelled anywhere in it: a comment is inert to the loader
  but not to a person or a script grepping the built exe for it, and a
  documented absence that answers "present" to every search is worse than no
  documentation. Searching the exe for that element must find nothing.

  Search for it case sensitively, in the lower case spelling a manifest uses.
  That count is 0, in this file and in the exe built from it, and it is the
  question this paragraph is answering. The first reading of this was taken
  with a case sensitive grep and reported as a plain 0, which was true and told
  the reader less than it seemed to.

  A case insensitive search is a different question and does not answer 0.
  Every hit is the awareness *call*, which is capitalised differently and is
  meant to be there: the call in main.rs, its import and symbol names, and this
  paragraph, which ships inside the exe with the rest of the file.

  That question is not answered with a number here any more, and there are two
  reasons. It used to say 7. On one tree, at one moment, both of these are
  true:

      debug build     7
      release build   4

  so the figure was never a property of the source. It is a property of an
  artefact, and neither reading recorded which artefact it came from, which is
  the whole of the disagreement. And it is self referential: this paragraph is
  one of the things being counted, so writing the number down changes it.

  What can be checked from a checkout is the rule, not the tally, and
  `windows/tools/manifest-parses.py` checks it: the lower case spelling appears
  in no manifest here. The command for the artefact side is in that file too.

  The rule this paragraph nearly broke: it must not spell the element itself.
  A draft of it did, to be helpful, and the built exe promptly answered
  "present" to the very search described two lines above.

  One rule for anyone editing this comment: **it must never contain two
  hyphens in a row.** XML forbids that sequence inside a comment, so the
  house dash used everywhere else in this port makes this file fail to parse
  and the exe that embeds it fail to start, with nothing at build time saying
  so. That is not hypothetical: it is what this comment did. `build.rs`
  parses this file before embedding it, so a slip fails the build instead.
-->
<assembly xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1" manifestVersion="1.0">
  <assemblyIdentity type="win32" name="Polter.Host" version="1.0.0.0" processorArchitecture="amd64"/>
  <dependency>
    <dependentAssembly>
      <assemblyIdentity
        type="win32"
        name="Microsoft.Windows.Common-Controls"
        version="6.0.0.0"
        processorArchitecture="*"
        publicKeyToken="6595b64144ccf1df"
        language="*"/>
    </dependentAssembly>
  </dependency>
</assembly>
